en.description:
Decide whether a string reads as a decimal number. A valid number consists of, in order:
1. an optional sign, + or -;
2. an integer or a decimal. An integer is one or more digits; a decimal is "digits, a point, digits" with at least one digit on some side of the point (so 3., .5 and 3.14 are fine, a lone . is not);
3. an optional exponent: the letter e or E followed by an integer, which may carry one sign + or - and has at least one digit.
Nothing else may appear. For example +100, 5e2, -123, 3.1416, -1E-16, 0123, .1 and 7. are numbers; 12e, 1a3.14, 1.2.3, +-5, 12e+5.4, e9 and inf are not.

Source: Coding Interviews (剑指 Offer, 2nd ed.), Offer 20. Statement, examples and tests written by CSWork.

Implement an integer queue with three operations:
push x: put x at the back;
pop: remove and print the front element, or print -1 if the queue is empty;
max: print the largest value now in the queue, or -1 if it is empty.
Run the given operations in order; each should take O(1) amortized time.

Source: Coding Interviews (剑指 Offer, 2nd ed.), Offer 59-II. Statement, examples and tests written by CSWork.

Use one array of length 3c to run three stacks numbered 0, 1 and 2, each holding at most c elements. Support:
push s v: push v onto stack s; if that stack is full, ignore the operation;
pop s: pop and print the top of stack s, or -1 if it is empty;
peek s: print the top of stack s without removing it, or -1 if it is empty;
empty s: print true if stack s is empty, otherwise false.

Source: Cracking the Coding Interview (6th ed.), CtCI 03.01. Statement, examples and tests written by CSWork.

A pile of plates topples if it grows too tall, so each pile holds at most cap plates and a new pile starts when the last is full. Number the piles 0, 1, 2, … from left to right and implement:
push v: put plate v on the rightmost pile, starting a new pile on the right if there is none or the rightmost is full;
pop: take the top plate off the rightmost pile and print it, or -1 if there are no plates;
popAt i: take the top plate off pile i and print it, or -1 if there is no pile i.
A pile that becomes empty is removed at once, and the piles to its right shift down by one. cap may be 0, in which case nothing can be stacked.

Source: Cracking the Coding Interview (6th ed.), CtCI 03.03. Statement, examples and tests written by CSWork.

Design a special stack whose top is always the smallest element, whatever order elements arrive in. Support:
push v: add v;
pop: remove the top (the smallest element); do nothing and print nothing when empty;
peek: print the top, or -1 when empty;
isEmpty: print true when empty, otherwise false.
The book asks you to sort using just one extra stack; that is not enforced here.

Source: Cracking the Coding Interview (6th ed.), CtCI 03.05. Statement, examples and tests written by CSWork.

A shelter takes only cats and dogs and works strictly first in, first out: an adopter may ask for the animal that has waited longest (cat or dog), or name cat or dog and get the longest-waiting one of that kind. Every animal has a distinct id, unrelated to arrival order. Implement:
enqueue id k: animal id arrives, k = 0 for a cat and k = 1 for a dog;
dequeueAny: adopt the animal that arrived first;
dequeueDog: adopt the dog that arrived first;
dequeueCat: adopt the cat that arrived first.
For each adoption print "id k" of the adopted animal, or -1 -1 when none fits.

Source: Cracking the Coding Interview (6th ed.), CtCI 03.06. Statement, examples and tests written by CSWork.
